od law of a harmony on two opposed  elements and the elements were physical  things like the hot and the cold of the  white and the dry or the bitter and the  sweet and there might be a whole bunch  of these eric Senecas is not actually  trying to give us an exhaustive medical  treatise at this point or outline you  know what the whole metaphysics of the  system is but do you think that medicine  is able to do what it does by  introducing good love good love is a  kind of harmony a kind of Concord  between the elements that he brings up  Heraclitus and he says Heraclitus has  this interesting passage  Heraclitus by the way is often called  Heraclitus the obscure because in part  we don't have that much of his work and  in part because he would say things that  were seen as enigmatic so Heraclitus  talks about the one that's in conflict  in itself being held together like the  harmony of the bow and the wire so he's  talking about something else perhaps a  cosmic principle and then he's likening  it to the harmony of the ball right bow  and arrow you stretch out the bow you  produce force in part by by pulling back  the the not the arrow the string is it  is in a certain way opposed to the bow  itself the arm is being opposed to the  bow and that's what allow something to  happen that's what allows the motion to  take place something that the the Greeks  are very familiar with also the wire so  the lyre is one of the important early  instruments and it's got multiple  strings it doesn't have France or  anything like that that's another way to  make multiple sounds out of an  instrument right instead it gives a  whole sequence of strings and you play  the string that you want to hear another  instrument that's very similar would be  the harp now is the lyre in harmony with  itself in the same way that the bow is  no not exactly the same way there's  there's a different type of metaphor  here  you could create a harmony in part by  playing what we nowadays call an  arp